{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2448 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "cdd8512cd6b707dd93433833d6a717aa96a9902a6c0460ddbf065a6c5d1b206f" :edb/db-before-t 2447 :edb/db-after-t 2448 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535760 50 #inst "2026-10-09T06:41:30.106Z" 13194139535760 true] [17592186151890 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n2x^2 y'' + 6 xy' + 2y = 0, \\qquad x>0.\n$$" 13194139535760 true] [17592186151890 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n$2x^2 y'' + 6 xy' + 2y = 0, \\qquad x>0$." 13194139535760 false] [17592186151890 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form\n\n$$\ny=x^\\lambda\n$$\n\nDifferentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} \\cdot λ(λ - 1)x^{λ - 2} + 6x \\cdot λx^{λ - 1} + 2x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\n2λ(λ - 1)x^{λ} + 6λx^{λ} + 2x^{λ} &= 0 \\\\\nx^{λ}[2λ(λ - 1) + 6λ + 2] &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2λ(λ - 1) + 6λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2λ^{2} + 4λ + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2(λ^{2} + 2λ + 1) &= 0 \\\\\n2(λ + 1)^{2} &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation has a double root\n\n$$\n\\lambda = -1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny(x) &= c_{1}x^{-1} + c_{2}x^{-1}\\ln x \\\\\n&= \\frac{c_{1}}{x} + \\frac{c_{2}\\ln x}{x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535760 false] [17592186151890 1145 "This is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume the solutions take the form $y=x^\\lambda.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\ny' = \\lambda x^{\\lambda -1}, \\qquad y'' = \\lambda(\\lambda-1)x^{\\lambda-2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}+6x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ 2\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }+6\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+2{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+6\\lambda +2] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following characteristic equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\lambda (\\lambda -1)+6\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ 2{\\lambda }^{2}+4\\lambda +2 & =0 \\\\ 2({\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +1) & =0 \\\\ 2(\\lambda +1)^{2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation has a double root $\\lambda = -1.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={c}_{1}{x}^{-1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{{c}_{1}}{x}+\\frac{{c}_{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535760 true] [17592186151891 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny =c_1 x^{10}+ c_2x ^{10}\\ln x ,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\nx^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0,\\quad  y'(1)=1, \\quad x>0.\n$$" 13194139535760 true] [17592186151891 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$x^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$y =c_1 x^{10}+ c_2x ^{10}\\ln x$,\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$x^2 y'' - 19 xy' + 100y = 0, \\quad y(1) = 0,\\quad y'(1)=1, \\quad x>0$." 13194139535760 false] [17592186151891 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0$. Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n$0= c_1$.\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 1$. Differentiating $y$, we get\n$y^{′}|= 10c_{1}x^{9} + c_{2}x^{9} + 10c_{2}x^{9}\\ln x$.\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=1$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 10c_{1} + c_{2} \\\\\n1 &= 0 + c_{2} \\\\\nc_{2} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = x ^{10}\\ln x\n$$" 13194139535760 false] [17592186151891 1145 "We find the constants $c_1$ and $c_2$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=0.$ Substituting $y(1)= 0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n0= c_1.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition $y'(1) = 1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =10{c}_{1}{x}^{9}+{c}_{2}{x}^{9}+10{c}_{2}{x}^{9}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $y'(1)=1$ and $c_1 = 0$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =10{c}_{1}+{c}_{2} \\\\ 1 & =0+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny(x) = x ^{10}\\ln x .\n$$" 13194139535760 true] [17592186152093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365039 13194139535760 true] [17592186152093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365046 13194139535760 true] [17592186152093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365053 13194139535760 true] [17592186152093 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365060 13194139535760 true] [17592186152094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365032 13194139535760 true] [17592186152094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365067 13194139535760 true] [17592186152094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365074 13194139535760 true] [17592186152094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365081 13194139535760 true] [17592186152094 1020 #edb/ref 17592186365088 13194139535760 true] [17592186152529 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535760 true] [17592186152529 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139535760 true] [17592186152529 1100 "Find the general solution to the equation\n\n$x^{2}y^{″} - xy^{′} + y = 0, x > 0$." 13194139535760 false] [17592186152529 1145 "The given equation is an instance of the Cauchy-Euler equation. So, we assume that the solutions take the form $y={x}^{\\lambda }.$ Differentiating $y$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1},\\,{y}^{″}=\\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above to our differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}\\cdot \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda -2}-x\\cdot \\lambda {x}^{\\lambda -1}+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ \\lambda (\\lambda -1){x}^{\\lambda }-\\lambda {x}^{\\lambda }+{x}^{\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ {x}^{\\lambda }[\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda (\\lambda -1)-\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +1 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -1)^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we have a double root $\\lambda =1.$\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)={c}_{1}{x}^{1}+{c}_{2}{x}^{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={c}_{1}x+{c}_{2}x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\n$$" 13194139535760 true] [17592186365032 1017 #edb/ref 17592186365033 13194139535760 true] [17592186365032 1089 "q-102365" 13194139535760 true] [17592186365032 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535760 true] [17592186365032 1100 "Given that the differential equation\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+15x{y}^{′}+49y=0\n$$\n\nhas the general solution\n\n$$\ny={c}_{1}{x}^{-7}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,\n$$\n\nsolve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}{y}^{″}+15x{y}^{′}+49y=0,\\,y(1)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(1)=1,\\,x>0.\n$$" 13194139535760 true] [17592186365032 1113 #uuid "b2f1e33b-97ff-5879-aa46-ccc76aa7159d" 13194139535760 true] [17592186365032 1145 "We find the constants ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$ using the initial conditions.\n\n- First, we apply the condition $y(1)=1.$ Substituting $y(1)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n1={c}_{1}.\n$$\n\n- Then, we apply the condition ${y}^{′}(1)=1.$ Differentiating $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =-7{c}_{1}{x}^{-8}+{c}_{2}{x}^{-8}-7{c}_{2}{x}^{-8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${y}^{′}(1)=1$ and ${c}_{1}=1$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =-7(1)+{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{2} & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(x) & ={x}^{-7}+8{x}^{-7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\  & =\\frac{1+8\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{x}^{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535760 true]